\subsection{Study coding and parameter-selective study differences}

Persons are nested in primary studies, and primary studies may differ in
initial conditions, diffusion, intercepts, or dynamic effects. CoTiMA therefore
represents study membership by $k-1$ time-independent predictors for $k$
studies. The argument \code{studyCoding} determines the contrast basis, while
\code{studyVarying} determines which parameter-matrix families receive those
study effects. These arguments apply identically to Stan- and Julia-based
fitting.

The default specification is

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  studyCoding = "dummy",
  studyVarying = c("CINT", "DIFFUSION", "T0VAR")
)
\end{lstlisting}

Dummy coding uses a reference study. Changing the reference does not change an
algebraically identical model's likelihood optimum, but it changes the raw
coordinates, starting values, and numerical scaling. A large and informative
reference study may therefore facilitate convergence. Weighted effect coding
instead defines the population coefficient as a sample-size weighted study
average:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  studyCoding = "weighted",
  studyVarying = c("CINT", "DIFFUSION", "T0VAR")
)
\end{lstlisting}

The coding changes the parameterization, not the information contained in the
data. It can improve numerical conditioning but cannot repair weak
identification. Study indicators model systematic between-study differences;
they do not by themselves create cluster-robust or hierarchical standard
errors. Hierarchical random-effects models require an additional study-level
distribution.

DRIFT remains invariant unless it is requested explicitly:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  studyCoding = "weighted",
  studyVarying = c("CINT", "DIFFUSION", "T0VAR", "DRIFT"),
  invariantDrift = "none"
)
\end{lstlisting}

The older argument \code{WEC=TRUE} is deprecated. During the transition it is
interpreted as \code{studyCoding="weighted"} and
\code{studyVarying=c("CINT", "DIFFUSION", "T0VAR")}. New analyses should use
the explicit arguments. Because the historical WEC implementation also varied
DRIFT parameters, old and new specifications are not necessarily the same
model. Analyses of residual DRIFT heterogeneity with \code{ctmaRedHet} must add
\code{"DRIFT"} to \code{studyVarying} explicitly.

\subsubsection{A controlled comparison of dummy and weighted coding}

Changing the coding can alter numerical conditioning even when both
parameterizations describe the same model. The function
\code{ctmaStudyCodingBenchmark} conducts a controlled comparison by fitting the
dummy-coded and weighted-coded versions with the same data, model arguments,
Stan controls, number of chains, and random seed. It is intended as a
computational diagnostic before committing resources to a long Bayesian fit.
The two fits are run sequentially, so the required wall-clock time is the sum
of both pilot fits.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
coding_benchmark <- ctmaStudyCodingBenchmark(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  studyVarying = c("DIFFUSION", "T0VAR"),
  fitArgs = list(
    binaries = c(1, 0),
    randomIntercepts = "CINT",
    scaleTime = 1 / 12
  ),
  iter = 200L,
  warmup = 150L,
  chains = 4L,
  coresToUse = 4L,
  seed = 20260907L,
  adapt_delta = 0.80,
  max_treedepth = 10L,
  retain_fits = FALSE
)

coding_benchmark
coding_benchmark$comparison
coding_benchmark$weighted_speedup_vs_dummy
\end{lstlisting}

The comparison reports elapsed time per condition and per post-warmup
iteration, divergences, maximum-tree-depth events, leapfrog effort, acceptance,
$\widehat{R}$, and effective sample size. A value of 3 for
\code{weighted\_speedup\_vs\_dummy}, for example, means that weighted coding was
three times faster in that matched pilot. Frequent maximum-tree-depth events
indicate difficult posterior geometry even in the absence of divergences.
Short pilots often have poor $\widehat{R}$ and effective sample sizes and must
not be used for substantive inference.

The named list \code{fitArgs} is the appropriate place for model-specific
arguments. Supplying them once ensures that both coding conditions fit exactly
the same model. Controlled arguments such as \code{studyCoding}, \code{iter},
and \code{chains} cannot be included in \code{fitArgs}. Additional Stan options
can be supplied through \code{backendArgs}; the benchmark itself controls
\code{warmup}, \code{adapt\_delta}, and \code{max\_treedepth}. Because fitted Stan
objects can be large, \code{retain\_fits=FALSE} stores the comparison and
parameter summaries without retaining both complete fits.

Simulated studies with identical true effects provide a particularly clean
test of computational parameterization: dummy and weighted coding should not
produce systematic substantive differences. This does not test performance
under genuine between-study heterogeneity. A separate heterogeneous simulation
is required for that purpose.

\subsection{Partial CoTiMA (\code{ctmaFit})}

Figure 36 demonstrates some further possibilities for conducting a CoTiMA; additional capabilities are explained in Appendix B. The CoTiMA model specified in Figure 36 fixes the effect of V2toV1 to 0.0 (which we do not generally recommend - let the evidence decide rather theoretical expectations), by labeling the according drift \code{0} or \code{"}\code{0}\code{"}. Further, only the effect V1toV2 is invariant across primary studies as defined in the \code{invariantDrift} argument (which could be reasonable -- and which could be decided based upon a statistical test see Subsection 7.3). The estimated drift coefficients of this partial CoTiMA are shown in Figure 37.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH
CoTiMAPart134Inv3Fit_6 <- ctmaFit(ctmaInitFit = CoTiMAInitFit_6_new,
    drift = matrix(c("V1toV1", 0,
                     "V1toV2", "V2toV2"),
                   nrow = 2, ncol = 2, byrow = TRUE),
    invariantDrift = c("V1toV2"),
    coresToUse = 2)
saveRDS(CoTiMAPart134Inv3Fit_6, paste0(activeDirectory,"CoTiMAPart134Inv3Fit_6.rds"))
summary(CoTiMAPart134Inv3Fit_6)
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{36}\emph{.} A Partial CoTiMA with a Subset of Primary Studies, with One Cross Effect Fixed to 0.0 and Only One Effect Invariant Across Primary Studies (\code{ctmaFit})

