\subsection{CoTiMA with Equality Constraints (\code{ctmaFit}, \code{ctmaEqual}, \code{ctmaCompFit})}

A \emph{-2ll}\emph{ difference test} can be applied whenever researchers want to compare two model fits. Note, however, that the result is only valid if the two models are nested, that is, the second model is derived from the first model by constraining parameters. Such constraints are present, for example, if parameters are eliminated from a model by constraining them to be 0.0 (like demonstrated in Figure 36), or by constraining other parameters to be equal. To statistically test if two or more effects are equal is a bit complex and requires three steps: (1) ensuring correct coding (polarity), (2) fitting a partially invariant CoTiMA using \code{ctmaFit}, and (3) testing equality using \code{ctmaEqual}.

First, (1) one has to take care that the effects to be compared have equal signs. For example, consider a model with three latent variables such as demands, resources, and burnout. Work-related resources, such as supervisor support, can be supposed to reduce burnout whereas demands increase burnout. To compare the effect sizes, one would need to go back to square one and re-start the EPIC part of the workflow. When preparing the correlations with \code{ctmaEmpCov}, one would need to use the \code{recode} argument to recode supervisor support so that it becomes lack of supervisor support. Then, one has to use \code{ctmaInit} again for initial fitting.

In the second step (2), one could start testing the equality of the effect sizes of supervisor support and of demands on burnout. This requires two CoTiMAs to be performed. The first CoTiMA has to specify those two (or more) effects as invariant across studies that should be tested for equality in the subsequent step. This is done with \code{ctmaFit}. We call this the invariance model.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH
CoTiMAFullInv23Fit_6 <- ctmaFit(ctmaInitFit = CoTiMAInitFit_6_new,
    invariantDrift = c("V2toV1", "V1toV2"),
    coresToUse = 2)
saveRDS(CoTiMAFullInv23Fit_6, paste0(activeDirectory,
        "CoTiMAFullInv23Fit_6.rds"))
summary(CoTiMAFullInv23Fit_6)

CoTiMAFullInvEq23Fit_6 <- ctmaEqual(ctmaInvariantFit = CoTiMAFullInv23Fit_6,
    coresToUse = 2)
saveRDS(CoTiMAFullInvEq23Fit_6, paste0(activeDirectory,
        "CoTiMAFullInvEq23Fit_6.rds"))

summary(CoTiMAFullInvEq23Fit_6)
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{38}\emph{. }Two-Step Procedure for Testing the Equality of Two Cross Effects (\code{ctmaFit}, \code{ctmaEqual})

Third (3), the CoTiMA fit-object returned then serves as an argument for \code{ctmaEqual}. The code for Step 2 and 3 is shown in Figure 38, in which \emph{V1toV2} and \emph{V2toV1} are first declared to be invariant and then tested for equality. We do not display all estimated drift parameters returned from \code{summary(CoTiMAFullInv23}\code{Fit\_6)} in a Figure here because it is sufficient to note that \emph{V1toV2} = .0444, \emph{V2toV1} = .0307, \emph{-2ll} = 25253.17, and the number of estimated parameters = 50. \emph{V1toV2} and \emph{V2toV1} were the only parameters that were aggregated, that is, invariant across primary studies. This is recognized by \code{ctmaEqual}, which, in addition to their invariance, constrains \emph{V1toV2} and \emph{V2toV1} to be equal. We call this the \emph{equality model}. Again, we do not display all estimated drift parameters returned from \code{summary(CoTiMAFullEq23Fit\_6)} in a Figure here because it is sufficient to note that \emph{V1toV2} = \emph{V2toV1} = .0364, \emph{-2ll} = 25253.89, and the number of estimated parameters = 49.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

[1] "  ###   NEXT MODEL COMPARISON   ###"
[2] "Diff_Minus2LL:  0.719788864123984"
[3] "Diff_df (= Diff_n.params):  1"
[4] "prob:  0.396213174274355"
[5] "Message1:  A prob value < .05 indicates a significant difference."
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{39}\emph{.} Result of the \emph{-2ll }\emph{D}\emph{ifference }\emph{T}\emph{est} Comparing the Fit of the Invariance Model with the Fit of the Equality to Test if Two Cross Effects are Equal (\code{ctmaEqual})

The \emph{-2ll}\emph{ difference test} examines if the fit (\emph{-2ll}\emph{ value}) of the equality model is not statistically worse than the fit of the invariance model. If this would be the case, then the hypothesis that both effects are equal has to be rejected and the alternative hypothesis that one effect (V2toV1 in this example) is significantly larger than the other one (V1toV2 in this example), will be retained. The \emph{-}\emph{2ll}\emph{ difference test} is automatically performed by \code{ctmaEqual}, too, it is displayed at the end of the \code{summary(CoTiMAFullInv23Fit\_6)}, and it is shown in Figure 39. In our example, the \emph{-2ll}\emph{ difference test} was not significant. Thus, we could not reject the hypothesis that V1toV2 = V2toV1.

Finally, we shall mention the \code{ctmaCompFit} function that comes with the CoTiMA package. The \code{ctmaCompFit} function is automatically used by \code{ctmaEqual}. It can also be applied whenever researchers want to compare two model fits with a \emph{-}\emph{2ll}\emph{ difference test} by using \code{ctmaCompFit(CoTiMAFit1, CoTiMAFit2)}. Note, however, that the result is only valid if the two models are nested, that is, the second model is derived from the first model by constraining parameters. Such constraints are present, for example, if parameters are eliminated from a model by constraining them to be 0.0, or by constraining other parameters to be equal. The former is achieved by setting the desired drift effect to \code{"}\code{0}\code{"}, and the latter is achieved by assigning identical labels to the desired drift effects. This could be done with the \code{ctmaInit} and \code{ctmaFit} functions. For example, the argument\code{ drift = matrix(}\code{c(}\code{"}\code{V1toV1}\code{"}\code{, 0, 0, }\code{"}\code{V1toV1}\code{"}\code{),}\code{ nrow = 2, ncol = 2, byrow = TRUE)} could be used to fit a model that has no cross effects and equal auto effects. This model is nested in a full CoTiMA model because it is more constrained.

\subsection{Moderated CoTiMA (\code{ctmaFit})}

CoTiMA can handle multiple continuous moderators and multiple categorical moderators, however, it is not yet possible to mix categorical and continuous ones. In general, we recommend starting with a single moderator to foster understanding how they operate before analyzing multiple moderators combined.

Recalling from Figure 20 that we entered information about two moderators. The first was the type of burnout measure applied in a primary study, which was either exhaustion or emotional exhaustion, and which was a categorical moderator. If there were two or more categorical moderators, the moderator numbers and moderator names would have to be provided as vectors (e.g., \code{mod.number = c(1, 3)}, \code{mod.names = c}\code{(}\code{"}\code{Burnout Measure}\code{"}\code{, }\code{"}\code{Study Quality}\code{"}\code{)}). However, in the present example in Figure 40, we use the first potential moderator variable only (\code{mod.number = 1)}, which was categorial (\code{mod.type }\code{= }\code{"}\code{cat}\code{"}\code{)} representing two types of burnout measures (\code{mod.names = }\code{"}\code{Burnout Measure}\code{"}\code{)}. By default, CoTiMA does (!) standardize moderators from version 0.5.3 onwards. In the present example, we did overwrite the default by including the argument \code{scaleMod = FALSE}. Thus, the k - 1 dummy variables created from the k categories of the moderator variable use values 0 and 1.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH
CoTiMAMod1onFullFit_6 <- ctmaFit(ctmaInitFit = CoTiMAInitFit_6_new,
    mod.number = 1,
    mod.type = "cat",
    mod.names = "Burnout Measure",
    coresToUse = 2,
    scaleMod = FALSE)
summary(CoTiMAMod1onFullFit_6)
saveRDS(CoTiMAMod1onFullFit_6, paste0(activeDirectory,
        "CoTiMAMod1onFullFit_6.rds"))
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{40}\emph{.} A Full Moderated CoTiMA with a Single Categorical Moderator (\code{ctmaFit})

Part of the results are shown in Figure 41. The drift effects shown in the Section\code{ }\code{\$estimates} are those in the reference group, which is always the group with the smallest category number. In the present example, these are the primary studies for which the moderator value was 1 (and internally recoded to 0 by CoTiMA) meaning they used an emotional exhaustion scale to measure burnout.

The Section \code{\$mod.effects} in Figure 41 shows the effects belonging group with the 2nd category number. In case there were more categories, one would find here four additional rows starting with \code{"}\code{3}\code{(category value}\code{)}\code{"} etc. It is important to note that this section does not show the drift effects. Rather, it shows how for primary studies of this category, which used an exhaustion compared to emotional exhaustion scale to measure burnout, the drift effects change compared to the reference group. Neither auto effects nor cross effects were significantly affected by the type of burnout measure. Leaving lack of significance aside, the effect of demands on burnout (\emph{V1toV2}) was increased if an exhaustion scale was used in primary studies and the effect of burnout on demands (\emph{V2toV1}) was reduced if an exhaustion rather than emotional exhaustion scale was used. We call this a positive moderating effect and a negative moderating effect of the exhaustion scale, respectively\footnote{Plotting the moderator effects is straightforward because for each time interval the change in the drift parameter introduced by the moderator can be depicted as shown in Figure 42. However, summarizing the effect of a moderator in continuous time is not as straightforward because of the non-linearities involved. To do so, the moderator effect is \emph{linearized} at the mean of the drift effect, and this linearized effect is reported in the \code{\$mod.effects} section.}.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

