\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{41}\emph{.} Part of the Results Moderated Full CoTiMA (\code{ctmaFit})

As always, the sizes of continuous time effects are virtually impossible to interpret. For example, the effect \emph{V1toV2} is .0373 for emotional exhaustion and .0373 + .0173 = .0546 (linearized; see Footnote 13) for exhaustion. However, how these effects unfold over time also depends on the other three effects \emph{V1toV1}, \emph{V2toV2}, and \emph{V2toV1}. We used \code{plot(CoTiMAMod1onFullFit\_6, timeUnit = }\code{"}\code{Quarters}\code{", timeRange = c(1, 36, 1))} to plot the moderated discrete time effects. For \emph{V1toV2}, the course of the moderated effect over discrete time is shown in Figure 42.

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{figures/figure_05.png}
\end{center}

\noindent\emph{\emph{Figure }\emph{42}\emph{.} The Cross-Lagged Effect \emph{V1toV2} Moderated by Type of Burnout Measure \newline{}(R = Reference Category: Emotional Exhaustion, 2 = Exhaustion) from 1 to 36 Quarters (the Horizontal Location of the Category Indicators R (Reference Category) and 2 has no Inherent Meaning; \code{plot})}

Since only two categories exist, \code{catsToCompare = c(1, 2)} is the only viable option in the present example. In the fitted model the moderating effects of Category 1 and Category 2 are restricted to be invariant. If this assumption is valid (i.e., moderating effects are not different for the two categories), the \emph{-}\emph{2ll}\emph{ value} of the restricted model should not be significantly different from the \emph{-2ll}\emph{ value} of the unrestricted model. This is tested with the \code{ctmaCompFit} function at the bottom of Figure 43, which shows (not displayed in a figure) that the difference in the \emph{-2ll}\emph{ values }given 4 degrees of freedom is not significant (\(\Delta\)(\emph{-2ll}) = 5.3201; \(\Delta\)(\emph{df}) = 4; \emph{p} = 0.2560). In fact, with only two categories available, restricting their effects to be invariant is conceptually identical to assuming there is no moderating effect. Hence, comparing the (unrestricted) moderator model with the full CoTiMA model estimated earlier (which had not moderator effect included), should yield virtually identical results, and indeed \code{ctmaCompFit(CoTiMAFullFit\_6\_new,}\code{ }\code{CoTiMAMod1onFullFit\_6)} yields (\(\Delta\)(\emph{-2ll}) = 5.3201; \(\Delta\)(\emph{df}) = 4; \emph{p} = 0.2560). However, with three or more categories these two \emph{-2ll}\emph{ difference tests} will yield diverging results\footnote{Instead of \code{c}\code{(}\code{1}\code{, }\code{2}\code{)}, it would also be possible to use indices such as \code{c}\code{(}\code{i}\code{, }\code{j}\code{)} and then use a double loop in R to compare all possible combinations of categories. For example:\par \code{for}\code{(i\textasciitilde{}in\textasciitilde{}}\code{1}\code{:(numberOfCats-1))\textasciitilde{}\{\textasciitilde{}}\code{for}\code{(j\textasciitilde{}in\textasciitilde{}(}\code{i+}\code{1}\code{):numberOfCats)\textasciitilde{}\{}\par \code{\textasciitilde{}tmpFit\textasciitilde{}<-\textasciitilde{}}\code{ctmaFit}\code{(}\code{ctmaInitFit\textasciitilde{}}\code{=\textasciitilde{}CoTiMAInitFit\_object,\textasciitilde{}}\code{mod.number\textasciitilde{}}\code{=\textasciitilde{}}\code{1}\code{,}\par \code{\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}\textasciitilde{}}\code{mod.type\textasciitilde{}}\code{=\textasciitilde{}"}\code{cat}\code{",\textasciitilde{}}\code{catsToCompare\textasciitilde{}}\code{=\textasciitilde{}}\code{c}\code{(}\code{i}\code{,\textasciitilde{}}\code{j}\code{))}\par \code{saveRDS}\code{(tmpFit,\textasciitilde{}}\code{p}\code{aste0}\code{(activeDirectory,\textasciitilde{}"}\code{CoTiMAModFit\_cat}\code{",\textasciitilde{}i,\textasciitilde{}"}\code{\_}\code{",\textasciitilde{}j,\textasciitilde{}"}\code{.rds}\code{"))\}\}}}.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH
CoTiMAMod1onFullFit_6_cats12 <- ctmaFit(ctmaInitFit = CoTiMAInitFit_6_new,
    mod.number = 1,
    mod.type = "cat",
    mod.names = "Burnout Measure",
    catsToCompare = c(1,2),
    scaleMod = FALSE)
saveRDS(CoTiMAMod1onFullFit_6_cats12,
        paste0(activeDirectory, "CoTiMAMod1onFullFit_6_cats12.rds"))
ctmaCompFit(CoTiMAMod1onFullFit_6_cats12, CoTiMAMod1onFullFit_6)
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{43}\emph{.} Comparing the Effect of two Categories of a Categorical Moderator (\code{ctmaFit}, \code{ctmaCompFit})

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH
tmpStudyList <- ctmaPrep(selectedStudies = c(1, 4, 313, 18),
    activeDirectory = activeDirectory,
    addElements = c("demands", "burnout"),
    moderatorLabels = c("Burnout Measure", "Control at Work"),
    moderatorValues = list(c("1 = Emotional Exhaustion",
                    "2 = Exhaustion"),
                       "continuous"))

CoTiMAMod2on23Fit_6 <- ctmaFit(ctmaInitFit = CoTiMAInitFit_6_new,
    x = tmpStudyList,
    mod.number = 2,
    mod.type = "cont",
    mod.names = "Control",
    moderatedDrift = c("V1toV2", "V2toV1"),
    scaleMod = TRUE,
    coresToUse = 2)
summary(CoTiMAMod2on23Fit_6)
saveRDS(CoTiMAMod2on23Fit_6, paste0(activeDirectory,
        "CoTiMAMod2on23Fit_6.rds"))
plot(CoTiMAMod2on23Fit_6, timeUnit = "Quarters", timeRange = c(1, 36, 1))
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{44}\emph{.} A Partially Moderated CoTiMA with a Single Continuous Moderator (\code{ctmaFit})

The code for a partially moderated CoTiMA with a single \emph{continuous} moderator is shown in Figure 44. Again, the types of primary studies we use in our example impose a difficulty that is likely to occur in many practical circumstances: For some studies the moderator variable is not available, and the moderator was therefore coded as NA. In our example, this was the case for Study 201 and Study 32. However, instead of going back to square one and compiling a reduced study list followed by applying \code{ctmaInit }again, we create a temporary study list using \code{ctmaPrep,} which does no longer include Study 201 and Study 32 (\code{tmpStudyList}). We use this temporary study list to specify an optional argument of the \code{ctmaFit} function (i.e., \code{primaryStudyList = tmpStudyList}).

To conduct a moderated CoTiMA, further arguments have to be specified. In the current example in Figure 44 only the cross effects are specified to be moderated. It is recommended to standardize continuous moderators, which is achieved by \code{scaleMod = TRUE.}\code{ }When continuous moderators are standardized, the estimated drift parameters are those for a prototypical study with a mean moderator value (average effect). The summary (not shown) reveals that control does not significantly reduce \emph{V2toV1} (i.e., the moderating effect) by -.0379 from the average effect, which is \emph{V2toV1} = .0685 (i.e., the main effect).

The plot function shown in Figure 44 yields the plot shown in Figure 45. Across all time intervals, for people who have low levels of control at work, effects of demands on burnout are larger than for those with high levels of control. In most empirical articles that visualize moderator effects for moderator values at +2\emph{SD} and -2\emph{SD} are not shown. This could be achieved by using \code{mod.values = c(-1, 0, 1)} as additional argument for the plot function in Figure 44.

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{figures/figure_06.png}
\end{center}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{45}\emph{.} The Cross-Lagged Effect \emph{V2toV1} Moderated (not Significantly) by Control at Work from 1 to 36 Quarters. The Lines show the Effect of \emph{V2toV1} for Control at -2\emph{SD} Below the Mean of Control (-2), -1\emph{SD} Below the Mean of Control (-1), at the Mean of Control (0), +1\emph{SD} above the Mean of Control (1), and +2\emph{SD} Above the Mean of Control (2). The Horizontal Location of the \emph{SD} Values has no Inherent Meaning.

\section{Bias \& Generalizability (\code{ctmaBiG})}

After finishing the EPIC part of the EPIC-BiG-Power workflow, we can now turn to the first part of the \textbf{BiG} workflow, which is done by using \code{ctmaBiG}. It performs Egger's tests for drift coefficients (e.g., Sterne \& Egger, 2001) and provides PET-PEESE corrections of fixed effect estimates (Stanley \& Doucouliagos, 2014). Random effect estimates are also computed. Various measures of heterogeneity (cf. Borenstein et al., 2009) as well as measures of expected replications rates (ERR) and expected discovery rates (EDR; Bartoš \& Schimmack, 2022; Brunner \& Schimmack, 2020) are also provided by \code{ctmaBiG}. The return object of \code{ctmaBiG} can be used to plot funnel plots and forest plots.

To proceed with \code{ctmaBiG}, we use the \code{init} fit file and data of primary studies published in the online repository of Dormann et al. (2020), which belongs to their CoTiMA of job stressors and burnout. The file containing their \code{init} fit-object can be retrieved from the website of the Open Science Foundations with the code shown in Figure 46. Note that Guthier et al. (2020) used a preliminary CoTiMA version that was based on the OpenMx R-package (Boker et al., 2011), whereas the file we suggest downloading was created with the rstan R-package (Stan Development Team, 2020). The latter samples parameter estimates from generated parameter distribution and results thus slightly change from analysis to analysis (unless the argument \code{finishsamples} is set to a large value, e.g., 10000). So, one could expect minor differences compared to the results reported in Guthier et al. (2020). On the other hand, the \code{init} fit-object contains all information required to replicate all their results with minor deviations\footnote{In addition to the fitted ctsem models of each primary study, it is possible to extract all information from an \code{init} fit-object that were originally complied with \code{ctmaPrep} by, e.g., \code{originalStudyList} \code{<-} \code{initFitObject\$primaryStudyList}. Thus, replicability of CoTiMA results is easily enabled by making one's \code{init}\code{ }fit-object available for download in a repository, for example, using the Open Science Framework http://osf.io/}. Note, however, computations could last a few hours except \code{ctmaBiG}. This is the major reason why we did not use their \code{init} fit-object before.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH
dl_link <- "https://osf.io/download/qhpae/"
target_file <- paste0(activeDirectory, "CoTiMAInitFit_D_BO_stanct.rds")
download.file(dl_link, target_file) # Note on windows computers add mode="wb"
CoTiMAInitFit_D_BO <- readRDS(target_file)
saveRDS(CoTiMAInitFit_D_BO, paste0(activeDirectory, "CoTiMAInitFit_D_BO.rds"))
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{46}\emph{.} Downloading the \code{Init}-Fit File of Guthier et al. (2020)

The analysis of bias and generalizability, summarizing the results, and plotting forest plots and funnel plots is achieved with the code in Figure 47. First, results of fixed effects analyses of single drift coefficients are displayed. Recall that in CoTiMA all drift effects (full CoTiMA) or a subset (partial CoTiMA) is aggregated simultaneously, thereby taking the entire causal system into account. Thus, CoTiMA estimates a set of fixed effects by constraining a set of drift effects to be invariant across groups (i.e., primary studies). Estimation is based on minimization of the discrepancy between the model implied covariance matrices and their empirical counterparts.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH
CoTiMABiG_D_BO <- ctmaBiG(CoTiMAInitFit_D_BO)
summary(CoTiMABiG_D_BO)
plot(CoTiMABiG_D_BO, activeDirectory = activeDirectory)
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{47}\emph{.} Analysis of Bias and Generalizability, Summary of Results, and Plotting (\code{ctmaBiG})

Contrary, in terms of a traditional fixed and random effects analysis, the drift effects of all primary studies, which resulted from the initial fitting of ctsem models one by one rather than as a set, are analyzed. Estimation is based on the standard errors of the drift effects rather than on minimizing discrepancies between implied and empirical covariance matrices. The fixed effect estimates of the two cross effects reported in the section \code{\$`Fixed Effects of Drift Coefficients`} of Figure 48 were V1toV2 = .0024 (p < .001) and V2toV1 = .0053 (p < .001).

The next section in Figure 48 is \code{\$Heterogeneity}. Here \emph{\(\tau\)}\textsuperscript{\emph{2}} , \emph{H}\textsuperscript{\emph{2}} , and \emph{I}\textsuperscript{\emph{2}} are shown, of which \emph{I}\textsuperscript{\emph{2}} is usually of most interest. Note that estimates of \emph{\(\tau\)}\textsuperscript{\emph{2}} were small so even four decimal places are not sufficient to show this. Consequently, between-study heterogeneity as indicated by \emph{I}\textsuperscript{\emph{2}} was large with the exception of the (small) effect V1toV2.

The third section (\code{\$`Random Effects of Drift Coeffici}\code{en}\code{ts`}) in Figure 48 displays the random effect estimates, their SE, confidence intervals (\code{Limit}), and the z-values with their associated probability levels. In addition, prediction intervals (\code{LimitPI}) also allow assessing the degree of heterogeneity. Prediction intervals describe a region in which about 95\% of the true study effects are expected to be found (e.g., Guddat et al., 2012). The effects V1toV2 = .0061 (p < .001) and V2toV1 = .0114 (p < .001) were larger than their fixed effects counterparts reported earlier. Note that the corresponding CoTiMA (fixed) effects reported by Guthier et al. (2020) were V1toV2 = .0039 (p < .001) and V2toV1 = .0084 (p < .001), and they were right in the middle between the traditional fixed and random effects estimates.

