\section{Special Topics}

In Chapter 10, several special topics are discussed. We start in Section 10.1 with explaining how a CoTiMA fit-object could be used to go back to the P-Step of the EPIC-Big-Power Workflow and obtain the list of primary studies, which was once compiled using the \code{ctmaPrep} function. This could be useful for re-doing CoTiMAs with different setting across a team of authors because only the fit files need to be shared, and it is achieved by the \code{ctmaFitToPrep} function. One possible reason why researchers want to do this is because they want to apply a CoTiMA to a subset of studies, which we demonstrate in Section 10.2, where we also introduce random intercept models (RI-CoTiMA) and four different specifications how random intercept models can be fitted. Another good reason for re-doing CoTiMAs is to check if results are replicable -- sometimes initially obtained results differ slightly depending on the operating systems, time scaling used etc. Therefore, in Section 10.3, we continue with demonstrating how the user could test if a better fit than previously obtained could be achieved. This is useful to ensure that the previously obtained did not suffer from the fitting algorithm being stuck in a local minimum, and it is done with the function \code{ctmaOptimizeFit}. Optimizing model fit with \code{ctmaOptimizeFit} could take a lot of time. However, once an optimal fit is obtained, reproducibility of the optimal fit could be facilitated by extracting fitting parameters including starting values from the optimized fit, and by explicitly including them in subsequent (and fast) fitting attempts. This is demonstrated in Section 10.4. In Section 10.5, we deal with the relation between CoTiMAs with random intercepts (RI-CoTiMA) and latent change score (LCS) models and we show how the results of a RI-CoTiMA can be transformed into a continuous time dual latent change score meta-analysis using the \code{ctmaLCS} function.

\subsection{From Fit back to Prep (\code{ctmaFitToPrep})}

A fit-object created with \code{ctmaInit} or with \code{ctmaFit} could serve as argument of the \code{ctmaFitToPrep} function to recover the original list of primary studies, and to modify when necessary. The resulting object has one notable difference compared to the original study list created during the P-Step of the EPIC process: the summary function is not operational. In Figure 59, the previously (see Figure\emph{ }46) downloaded \code{i}\code{nit}  fit file created by Guthier et al. (2020) is used to create a new study list.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH
CoTiMAInitFit_D_BO <- readRDS(paste0(activeDirectory, "CoTiMAInitFit_D_BO.rds"))
studyList_D_BO <- ctmaFitToPrep(ctmaFitObject = CoTiMAInitFit_D_BO,
    reUseEmprawData = TRUE)
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{59}\emph{.} Using \code{ctmaFitToPrep} to Create a new Study List to be used as Input for Subsequent \code{ctmaInit}

The created study list (i.e., \code{studyList\_D\_BO}) could be directly used as argument for \code{ctmaInit} or for \code{ctmaFit}, or it could be modified before. This requires some R code and some experience with list objects in R. For example, the R code shown in Figure 61 could be used to delete all primary studies with two waves only. Further, Study 313 is also excluded even though it comprised three time points because it could not be fitted properly, which we explain further below. The code in Figure 60 generates the output shown in Figure 61.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

studies3plus <- lapply(studyList_D_BO$deltas, function(x) (length(x) > 1))
head(studies3plus)
targetPos <- which(unlist(studies3plus == TRUE)); targetPos
studyNumbers <- unlist(studyList_D_BO$studyNumbers); studyNumbers
targetStudies <- c(studyNumbers[targetPos], 313); targetStudies
excludedStudies <- studyNumbers[!(studyNumbers %in% targetStudies)]
excludedStudies
study3plusList <- ctmaPrep(ctmaPrepObject = studyList_D_BO,
    excludedStudies = excludedStudies)
unlist(study3plusList$studyNumbers)
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{60}\emph{.} R Code for Deleting all Studies with 2 Time Points from a \code{ctma}\code{Prep}-Object Using\textasciitilde{}\code{ctmaFitToPrep} to Create a New Study List to be used as Input for Subsequent\textasciitilde{}\code{ctmaInit}

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

# Step 1: Create a list containing a FALSE for every primary study with only one
# time interval
[[1]]
[1] FALSE
[[2]]
[1] FALSE
[[3]]
[1] FALSE
[[4]]
