\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{64}\emph{.} Corresponding R Output for the Code used in Figure 63 (\emph{S}\emph{uspicious}\emph{ }Estimates are Shown in Bold Face)

Despite the differences in \emph{-2ll values}, the estimates shown in Figure 64 were mostly consistent across the four models; in particular, this applies to the two models using randomly varying manifest means, which also had the best \emph{-2ll values}. Some \emph{supicious} estimates are marked in bold face. Our decision to mark effects as supicious was based on the size of effects, in particular we regarded large effects and standard errors in models with large \emph{-2ll values} as supicious. Overall, for a subsequent full CoTiMA, we should chose the \code{"}\code{MANIFEST}\code{"} options, which seems to work well for all primary studies, and we should use it together with the arugment \code{randomIntercepts}\code{ }\code{=}\code{ }\code{"}\code{MANIFEST}\code{"}\code{ } rather than with \code{indVarying}\code{,} which would be more restrictive.

Fitting a full CoTiMA with randomly varying manifest means is demonstrated in Figure 65. Not shown here, we obtained \emph{-2ll} = 44568.95 with 89 estimated parameters. More important, a warning was issued: \code{"}\code{Warning: ***Generalized inverse required for Hessian inversion -- standard errors not trustworthy}\code{"}. This indicates problems in proper model convergence. Section 10.3 shows how this and similar problems could be dealt with.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH
CoTiMAFullFit_D_BO_3plus_RI <-
    ctmaFit(activeDirectory = activeDirectory,
    ctmaInitFit = CoTiMAInitFit_D_BO_3plus_RI,
    scaleTime = 1/12, coresToUse = 2,
    randomIntercepts = "MANIFEST")
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{65}\emph{.} Fitting a Full CoTiMA with Randomly Varying Manifest Means (\code{ctma}\code{Fit}; \code{saveRDS} and \code{summary} Functions not Shown for Reasons of Space)

\subsection{Optimizing Fit (\code{ctma}\code{Optimize}\code{Fit})}

The CoTiMA package offers a couple of possibilities for automatically varying certain fitting parameters during a series of fit attempts. For example, the time scale chosen may not be well-suited for the build-in starting values, which may prevent proper convergence.

Automatically varying certain fitting parameters during a series of fit attempts possibilities are implemented in the function, \code{ctmaOptimizeFit}, which generates a series of model fits (either using \code{ctmaInit} or \code{ctmaFit}), in which different time scales could be used. For example, the argument \code{randomScaleTime = c(1/24, 1/2)}, would vary the original time scale in subsequent model fits by multiplying it with a randomly selected factor between \code{1/24} and \code{1/2}.

Whereas the argument \code{randomScaleTime} could be used both when optimizing using \code{ctmaInit} or \code{ctmaFit}, there are arguments that apply either to optimizing with \code{ctmaFit} or \code{ctmaInit}. A first, but not much obvious way to facilitate model fit, is to vary the order of primary studies that were previously used with \code{ctmaInit}. Recall that in case of \emph{k} primary studies, \emph{k} -- 1 dummy variables are internally used to control for the nested data structure (persons in studies). The last study in the list of \emph{k} studies is used as the reference study for which no dummy variable is created. With the argument \code{shuffleStudyList = TRUE} the original order of the primary studies could be randomly changed in subsequent fit attempts with \code{ctmaFit}, which may also facilitate model convergence. Note that this argument has no impact when a single model fit should be optimized with \code{ctmaInit}.

A second way to facilitate fit is to standardize the dummy variables (time independent predictors, TIpreds, in ctsem terminology), so that they are no longer 0 or 1, but rather 0 on average with a standard deviation = 1.0. While this will not change the interpretation of the estimated drift coefficients, it may facilitate model convergence, too, and it is achieved by \code{randomScaleT}\code{I}\code{ = TRUE}, which randomly switches between standardized and unstandardized dummy variables in subsequent fit attempts.

The \code{ctmaOptimizeFit} function could be used also to optimize the fit for a single primary study using \code{ctmaInit}. For this purpose, one could use the argument, for example, \code{problemStudy = 3}, which re-fits the third study in the study list subsequently using varying fitting parameters. We do not present this in a Figure here. Rather, we focus on optimizing a full CoTiMA.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH
CoTiMAFullFit_D_BO_3plus_RI_opt <-
    ctmaOptimizeFit(activeDirectory = activeDirectory,
    ctmaFitFit = CoTiMAFullFit_D_BO_3plus_RI,
    ctmaInitFit = CoTiMAInitFit_D_BO_3plus_RI,
    finishsamples = 10000, coresToUse = 2,
    randomScaleTime = c(1/24, 1/2),
    shuffleStudyList = TRUE,
    saveModelFits = "/OptFitFiles/3plusRIopt",
    reFits = 50)
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{66}\emph{.} Optimizing a Full CoTiMA with Random Intercepts Modelled as Randomly Varying Manifest Means (\code{ctma}\code{OptimizeFit}; \code{saveRDS} and \code{summary} Functions not Shown for Reasons of Space)

Figure 66 demonstrates how to optimize the full RI-CoTiMA fitted as shown in Figure 65. Note that the model setup is not changed, and the arguments (e.g., \code{randomIntercepts = }\code{"}\code{MANIFEST}\code{"}) is taken from the \code{ctmaF}\code{it}\code{F}\code{it} object provided. In Figure 66, we requested 10,000 finishsamples to get precise estimates, we let the time scale factor randomly vary between 1/24 and 1/2 (of the original time scale, which was 1 month), we shuffle the list of primary study in subsequent fit attempts, we re-fit the model 50 times, and each fit is saved for inspecting the respective estimates later. Without providing the \code{saveModelFits} argument, the \code{ctmaOptimizeFit} function would return only the estimates of the best fitting model. The \code{saveModelFits} argument also prevents that already computed fits are lost in case very poor fitting parameters were randomly chosen, which may cause the abortion of \code{ctmaOptimizeFit}\code{.}

The R code shown in Figure 67 can be used to compare \emph{-2ll values} and check for consistency of estimates for the optimized models re-fitted in Figure 70. Figure 68 shows the corresponding output. At the top of Figure 68, the rounded \emph{-2ll values} of all 50 fit-attempts are displayed. Seven attempts yielded \emph{-2ll values} < 44474. And the index (no.) of these fit-attempts is determined next. Then, the fit files of these indexed fit-attempts are read, the estimated drift effects are extracted, sorted, and displayed. There is very little variation in any of the four drift effects and their associated standard errors across the seven fit files. Hence, one would regard these results as trustworthy. Interesting to note in passing is the fact the neither of the two cross effects is significant, which would lead to the conclusion that once random intercepts are statistically modelled, there is no evidence for an effect of workload on exhaustion nor vice versa from exhaustion to workload.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

# Step 1: Display -2ll values of all 50 fit attempts
table(round(CoTiMAFullFit_D_BO_3plus_RI_opt$all_minus2ll,0))

# Step 2: Determine the index (no.) of the best fit attempts (here: -2ll < 44474)
index <- which(CoTiMAFullFit_D_BO_3plus_RI_opt$all_minus2ll < 44475)
index

# Step 3: The fit files of the indexed fit attempts are read, the estimated drift

# effects are extracted, sorted, and displayed.
activeDirectory <- "../../" # SET A VALID PATH

fits <- list()
for (i in 1:length(index)) { fits[[i]] <-
    readRDS(paste0(activeDirectory, "/OptFitFiles/3plusRIopt ", index[i], " .rds"))}
allDrift <- as.data.frame((do.call(
    rbind, lapply(fits, function(x) x$summary$estimates_original_time_scale[1:4,]))))
allDrift <-round(allDrift, 4)
allDrift[order(allDrift[,1], allDrift[,2], decreasing = FALSE),]
\end{lstlisting}

