\noindent \emph{Figure }\emph{68}\emph{.} Corresponding R Output for the Code used in Figure 67

Thus far, it was quite a long way from initial fitting with \code{ctmaInit} to get trustworthy results for a full RI-CoTiMA. With the open science-movement, however, not only replicability of findings but also reproducibility of results became important. Facilitating reproducibility is demonstrated next.

\subsection{Facilitating Reproducibility of Results by Providing Start Values}

Reproducibility of findings could be facilitated much by using the best fitting model so far, extract starting values and other fitting parameters, and provide them as argument to the \code{ctmaFit} function. How this can be done is shown in Figure 69.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]

# Step 1: Extracting modelling parameter scaleTime
scaleTime <- CoTiMAFullFit_D_BO_3plus_RI_opt$usedTimeScale; scaleTime

# Step 2: Extracting modelling parameter scaleTI
scaleTI <- CoTiMAFullFit_D_BO_3plus_RI_opt$usedScaleTI; scaleTI

# Step 3: Extracting starting values (inits)
inits <- CoTiMAFullFit_D_BO_3plus_RI_opt$bestFit$studyFitList$stanfit$rawest
round(inits, 4)

# Step 4: Create the object primaryStudyList, which is used in Figure 71 below
primaryStudyList <- CoTiMAFullFit_D_BO_3plus_RI_opt$usedStudyList
\end{lstlisting}

